WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:06.550
One of the challenges of face recognition is that you need to solve the one-shot learning problem.

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:09.870
What that means is that for most face recognition applications,

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:17.350
you need to be able to recognize a person given just one single image or given just one example of that person's face.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:23.270
Historically, deep learning algorithms don't work well if you have only one training example.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:28.510
Let's see an example of what this means and then talk about how to address this problem.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:34.070
Let's say you have a database of four pictures of employees in your organization.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:39.750
These are actually some of my colleagues at deeplearning.ai, Kian, Danielle, Eunice, and Tian.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:46.110
Now, let's say someone shows up at the office and they want to be led through the turnstile.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:52.470
What the system has to do is despite ever having seen only one image of Danielle,

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:55.470
to recognize that this is actually the same person.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:59.470
In contrast, if you see someone that's not in this database,

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:04.550
then it should recognize that this is not any of the four persons in the database.

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:11.350
In the one-shot learning problem, you have to learn from just one example to recognize the person again.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:18.990
You need this for most face recognition systems because you might have only one picture of each of your employees

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:22.030
or each of your team members in your employee database.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:29.470
One approach you could try is to input the image of the person, feed it to a conf net,

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:37.310
and have it output a label Y using a softmax unit with four outputs or maybe five outputs

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:41.550
corresponding to each of these four persons or none of the above.

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:44.150
That would be five outputs in a softmax.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:48.710
This really doesn't work well because if you have such a small training set,

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:53.910
it's really not enough to train a robust neural network for this task.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:57.070
Also, whatever new person joins your team,

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:02.990
so now you have five persons you need to recognize, so there should now be six outputs.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:05.830
Do you have to retrain the conf net every time?

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:07.910
That just doesn't seem like a good approach.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:12.150
To carry out face recognition, to carry out one-shot learning,

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:17.990
instead, to make this work, what you're going to do instead is learn a similarity function.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:23.830
In particular, you want a neural network to learn a function which is going to denote D,

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:30.510
which inputs two images and outputs the degree of difference between the two images.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:37.350
If the two images are of the same person, you want this to output a small number.

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:43.430
If the two images are of two very different people, you want it to output a large number.

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:47.870
During recognition time, if the degree of difference between them

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:54.230
is less than some threshold called tau, which is a hyperparameter,

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:58.910
then you would predict that these two pictures are of the same person,

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:05.030
and if it's greater than tau, you would predict that these are different persons.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:11.670
This is how you address the face verification problem.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:18.550
To use this for a recognition task, what you do is, given this new picture,

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:23.030
you would use this function D to compare these two images,

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:27.830
and maybe it outputs a very large number, let's say 10 for this example.

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:32.310
Then you compare this with the second image in your database,

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:36.950
and because these two are the same person, hopefully it outputs a very small number.

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:40.390
Then you do this for the other images in your database,

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:48.470
and so on, and based on this, you would figure out that this is actually that person,

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:49.910
which is Danielle.

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:53.030
In contrast, if someone not in your database shows up,

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:57.350
as you use the function D to make all of these pairwise comparisons,

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:02.870
hopefully D will output a very large number for all four pairwise comparisons,

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:07.350
and then you say that this is not any one of the four persons in the database.

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:11.670
Notice how this allows you to solve the one-shot learning problem.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:16.550
So long as you can learn this function D, which inputs a pair of images

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:20.470
and tells you basically if they're the same person or different persons,

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:23.670
then if you have someone new join your team,

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:28.870
you can add a fifth person to your database, and it just works fine.

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:34.790
So you've seen how learning this function D, which inputs two images,

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:37.910
allows you to address the one-shot learning problem.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:42.870
In the next video, let's take a look at how you can actually train in your network

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:44.870
to learn this function D.
